WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.690
LEIGHA JARETT: Over the past few
"BigQuery Spotlight" episodes,

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:06.750
we've walked you through
the fundamentals of BigQuery

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.110
to help you get started
architecting and administering

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.650
your warehouse.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.190
Today, we're going
beyond that initial setup

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.190
and talking about how you
can ensure that your BigQuery

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.310
environment stays cost
effective, performant,

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.020
and secure through
ongoing monitoring.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.002 --> 00:00:33.430
Monitoring your data warehouse
on a continuous basis

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.310
is important for
every organization.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.840
Let's walk through
a few key use cases

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.800
so that you can understand
the different data sources

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.920
available to monitor and
the types of questions you

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.830
might be asking for each one.

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:51.040
First up, cost optimization.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.710
One of the ways you can
monitor BigQuery spend

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.900
and Google Cloud
spend in general

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.600
is to leverage the
billing reports.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.660
You can analyze billing data
directly in the Google Cloud

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.930
Console or export it
back into BigQuery

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.130
to join it against
other data sets

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:09.970
and create your
own custom reports.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:14.050
Let's jump into the Cloud
Console to take a look.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.610
In the Reports tab
of the Billing page,

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.660
we can see a chart with our
cost per a particular billing

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:24.750
account broken down by project,
service, SKU, or even label

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.180
keys.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.340
Focusing on BigQuery
services, there

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.290
are a few different
actions you might

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.200
want to take using this data.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.510
First off, you can
see how much money

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:38.120
is being spent on BigQuery
across different teams

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:42.920
or projects and use this to set
up budgets and notifications so

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.100
that you or others on your team
know if spend is getting out

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.870
of hand.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.740
Or you can even set up
custom quotas for BigQuery

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.200
so that specific
users or projects

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.350
are limited in the amount
of data they can process.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.280
Next, you might want to
dig into the storage SKUs

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.690
to look at a breakdown of
active versus long-term storage.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:09.500
In case you don't already know,
BigQuery charges half the price

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.830
for data that has not
been modified in 90 days.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:16.250
This includes entire tables
or specific partitions

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:18.360
within a table.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.780
So this information
may be able to help

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:24.080
you modify the way you
update data or narrow down

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.870
tables that could be
partitioned to take advantage

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.950
of long-term storage.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.460
Another SKU you may
see in the billing data

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.680
is for streaming inserts.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.230
While BigQuery charges
for streaming inserts,

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.280
batch loading is free.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.940
So if data being
streamed into BigQuery

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.370
doesn't need to be
consumed right away,

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:46.710
you might consider editing
your loading process.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.530
Finally, the billing
reports can be

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.050
super helpful for
pinpointing projects that

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:53.780
have spikes in analysis cost.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.900
Here, analysis means
on-demand querying,

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.140
where you're charged for
the amount of bytes scanned.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.710
There might be an opportunity
to apply a slot reservation

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:08.720
to these projects to save costs
and ensure predictable pricing.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.390
If you're not familiar
with BigQuery reservation

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:14.060
or BigQuery pricing
model, I highly

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.240
recommend that you go and visit
our "Jobs and Reservations"

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.350
video linked below.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:22.100
Now, if you do decide to
go with flat-rate pricing,

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.920
you'll need to figure out
what reservation size makes

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.710
the most sense for your needs.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:30.460
This brings us to
our second use case--

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:34.330
workload management and
performance monitoring.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:36.520
You can see how many
slots were consumed

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.280
across different workloads
in your organization

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.910
using the Cloud Monitoring
interface or the information

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:46.240
schema tables natively
available in BigQuery.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.150
For example, if your
historical slot consumptions

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:52.180
looks like this, you may
want to find the baseline

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.310
and purchase a
monthly or annual slot

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:58.990
commitment for that amount, in
this case, around 500 slots.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.120
For queries that are
causing those peaks,

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.210
you could choose to purchase
a flex-slot commitment,

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:07.300
run the query, and then
cancel the commitment.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.450
And as a reminder, the
number of available slots

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.370
is kind of like a
measure of throughput.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:15.610
This means that if less
slots are available,

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.550
the query will likely
just run slower.

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:20.680
You can always test
out query performance

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.260
with different number of
slots to find the size that

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:26.110
works best for your needs.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:29.230
Besides just determining the
size of your reservation,

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.830
you may also want to use Cloud
Monitoring or the information

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:36.100
schema to monitor
job performance.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.740
And actually, if you're using
slot reservations already,

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:43.600
you can also use the new
BigQuery Admin Resource Charts.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:45.970
Let's take a look.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.100
This data can help
you pinpoint queries

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:51.460
that should be optimized,
like this scheduled query that

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:54.610
seems to be consuming
a lot of resources.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:58.570
With the job ID, you can even
get the execution details

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:00.850
and use some of the
optimization techniques

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:03.820
we covered in our previous
video to figure out

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.900
if you can rewrite the
SQL to be more efficient.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:10.720
You might also want to
dig into job concurrency

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.620
to rearrange scheduling for
optimal slot utilization

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:16.720
or fail jobs to
help troubleshoot

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.240
any ongoing issues.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:20.540
OK, great.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:23.470
So we've reviewed some ways
to save money and optimize

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:25.280
performance.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.170
Now let's talk about how you can
audit data sharing and access

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:32.800
using Cloud Logging, which
allows you to analyze system

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.440
and application log data.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.720
And just like our
other data sources,

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:40.390
logs can be analyzed
in the console

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:43.330
or exported into BigQuery.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:45.340
In one of our
previous videos, we

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.740
talked about data governance.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:51.220
You might remember that we use
data catalog to develop policy

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:54.010
tags for controlling access.

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:55.870
In Cloud Logging,
we can do things

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.600
like find the email address
for the principle that

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.780
grants or removes
access on a policy tag

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:04.840
or the email address for
whom the access was granted

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.060
or removed.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:10.630
You can also directly see what
user identities have queried

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:15.010
which data sources, to ensure
that all access policies are

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:19.730
correct and no one has access
to data that they shouldn't.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:22.790
As you can see, monitoring
is essential to making

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:27.020
informed decisions on optimizing
your BigQuery data warehouse.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:29.630
As a quick review, you
can use the billing data

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:32.000
to create budgets,
custom quotas,

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.390
and dig into areas where
you can reduce costs

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:38.720
To size reservations, improve
performance, and troubleshoot

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.230
errors, you can use Cloud
Monitoring or the information

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:44.600
schema, or if
you're a reservation

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:48.860
customer, the new BigQuery
Admin Resource Charts.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:50.870
Finally, you can
use Cloud Logging

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.240
to review granular
log data, which

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:55.430
also might be useful
for troubleshooting

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:59.150
and to monitor data
access policies.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:01.460
The great part of
monitoring in BigQuery

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:04.790
is that you have the flexibility
to export any of these data

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:09.640
sources back into your data
warehouse for custom reporting.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.140
In fact, we've even built
a series of Data Studio

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.270
and Looker templates
to accelerate

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:17.790
any additional
analysis you want to do

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:22.290
on top of billing, information
schema, or log data.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:25.170
Make sure to check out the
links below for details,

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:28.950
and follow me on social to get
more BigQuery tips and tricks

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:30.990
Oh, and remember, stay curious.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:34.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

